sections.s2_technical:
The Spring Data repository hierarchy has five levels. Each adds more methods:

```java
// Level 1: Repository<T, ID> — marker interface, no methods
// Level 2: CrudRepository<T, ID> — basic CRUD
//   save(entity), saveAll(entities)
//   findById(id) → Optional<T>, findAllById(ids) → Iterable<T>
//   findAll() → Iterable<T>
//   count(), existsById(id)
//   deleteById(id), delete(entity), deleteAll()

// Level 3: ListCrudRepository<T, ID> — same as CrudRepository but returns List not Iterable
//   findAll() → List<T> instead of Iterable<T>
//   findAllById(ids) → List<T>

// Level 4: PagingAndSortingRepository<T, ID> — adds pagination and sorting
//   findAll(Sort sort) → Iterable<T>
//   findAll(Pageable pageable) → Page<T>

// Level 5: JpaRepository<T, ID> — adds JPA-specific features
//   flush()
//   saveAndFlush(entity) → T
//   saveAllAndFlush(entities) → List<T>
//   deleteAllInBatch()  — bulk DELETE without loading entities (one SQL)
//   deleteAllByIdInBatch(ids) — same for specific IDs
//   getReferenceById(id) — lazy proxy (no DB hit)
//   findAll() → List<T>, findAll(Sort) → List<T>
//   findAll(Example<T>) → List<T> — Query by Example
```

The key differences in practice:

```java
// ═══ CrudRepository — basic, tech-agnostic ═══
public interface AccountRepository extends CrudRepository<Account, String> {
    // findAll() returns Iterable<Account> — need to convert to List!
    // NO pagination methods
    // NO flush/saveAndFlush
    // NO batch delete
}

// Usage:
Iterable<Account> iterable = accountRepo.findAll();  // Iterable, not List!
List<Account> list = StreamSupport.stream(iterable.spliterator(), false).toList();  // verbose!

// ═══ JpaRepository — full-featured ═══
public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {
    // findAll() returns List<Account> — ready to use!
    // Pagination: findAll(PageRequest.of(0, 20))
    // Flush: saveAndFlush(account)
    // Batch: deleteAllInBatch()
    // Lazy: getReferenceById(id)
}

// Usage:
List<Account> list = accountRepo.findAll();  // List directly!
Page<Account> page = accountRepo.findAll(PageRequest.of(0, 20, Sort.by("balance").descending()));
accountRepo.saveAndFlush(account);  // flush immediately
accountRepo.deleteAllInBatch();     // DELETE FROM accounts — one SQL, no entity loading
```

`deleteAllInBatch` vs `deleteAll`: `deleteAll` loads ALL entities first, then deletes each one by one (N SELECT + N DELETE queries). `deleteAllInBatch` executes a single `DELETE FROM table` SQL — much faster for large datasets but does NOT trigger JPA lifecycle callbacks (@PreRemove).

```java
// deleteAll(): SELECT all → N × DELETE WHERE id=? (SLOW for 10K records)
accountRepo.deleteAll();

// deleteAllInBatch(): DELETE FROM accounts (ONE SQL, FAST, skips callbacks)
accountRepo.deleteAllInBatch();
```

